Avatar Design Explanation:
The avatar was implemented using the state design pattern to manage different states of the avatar,
such as idle, running, and jumping.
The main Avatar class represents the player entity and delegates behavior to movement
state objects that implement a common MovementState interface.
The following state classes were created:
- IdleState – handles standing still and passive energy recovery.
- RunState – handles horizontal movement and continuous energy consumption.
- JumpState – handles jumping, double-jumping logic, and mid-air movement.
State transitions are triggered by input and physical conditions:
- From IdleState to RunState when left/right keys are pressed and energy is sufficient.
- From IdleState or RunState to JumpState when space is pressed and the avatar is
on the ground and energy is sufficient.
- From JumpState to IdleState or RunState when the avatar lands, depending on horizontal input.
Transitions are handled explicitly inside each state, making the logic easy to follow and extend.
Energy Package Design:
The energy system was implemented as a dedicated package (pepse.world.avatar.energy).
This package contains three main components:
- Energy – a logic-only class responsible for managing the avatar’s energy value,
including consumption (running, jumping, double-jumping), recovery, and enforcing valid bounds.
- EnergyChangeListener – an interface that defines a callback (onEnergyChange) for
notifying interested components when the energy value changes.
- EnergyDisplay – a UI class that updates the on-screen energy
display only when notified of a change.
The energy logic remains independent of rendering concerns,
and the UI updates efficiently without polling the energy value every frame.

Trees Design Explanation:
The tree system is composed of multiple focused classes:
- Flora – responsible for generating trees in a given world range.
- Tree – a composite object that creates and aggregates trunk, leaves, and fruits.
- Trunk, Leaf, Fruit – individual GameObject subclasses representing tree parts.
Flora does not directly interact with the game world.
Instead, it returns a list of GameObjects, keeping world management inside the game manager.